{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,24]],"date-time":"2025-12-24T12:21:03Z","timestamp":1766578863996,"version":"3.40.5"},"reference-count":47,"publisher":"Public Library of Science (PLoS)","issue":"6","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2024,6,21]],"date-time":"2024-06-21T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1718928000000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"}],"content-domain":{"domain":["www.plosone.org"],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["PLoS ONE"],"abstract":"<jats:p>Block cipher is a cryptographic field that is now widely applied in various domains. Besides its security, deployment issues, implementation costs, and flexibility across different platforms are also crucial in practice. From an efficiency perspective, the linear layer is often the slowest transformation and requires significant implementation costs in block ciphers. Many current works employ lookup table techniques for linear layers, but they are quite costly and do not save memory storage space for the lookup tables. In this paper, we propose a novel lookup table technique to reduce memory storage when executing software. This technique is applied to the linear layer of block ciphers with recursive Maximum Distance Separable (MDS) matrices, Hadamard MDS matrices, and circulant MDS matrices of considerable sizes (e.g. sizes of 16, 32, 64, and so on). The proposed lookup table technique leverages the recursive property of linear matrices and the similarity in elements of Hadamard or circulant MDS matrices, allowing the construction of a lookup table for a submatrix instead of the entire linear matrix. The proposed lookup table technique enables the execution of the diffusion layer with unchanged computational complexity (number of XOR operations and memory accesses) compared to conventional lookup table implementations but allows a substantial reduction in memory storage for the pre-computed tables, potentially reducing the storage needed by 4 or 8 times or more. The memory storage will be reduced even more as the size of the MDS matrix increases. For instance, analysis shows that when the matrix size is 64, the memory storage ratio with the proposed lookup table technique decreases by 87.5% compared to the conventional lookup table technique. This method also allows for more flexible software implementations of large-sized linear layers across different environments.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1371\/journal.pone.0304873","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2024,6,21]],"date-time":"2024-06-21T17:38:48Z","timestamp":1718991528000},"page":"e0304873","update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1371\/journal.pone.corrections_policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":1,"title":["Efficient implementation of the linear layer of block ciphers with large MDS matrices based on a new lookup table technique"],"prefix":"10.1371","volume":"19","author":[{"given":"Tran Thi","family":"Luong","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Nguyen","family":"Van Long","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2723-1138","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Bay","family":"Vo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"340","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2024,6,21]]},"reference":[{"issue":"4","key":"pone.0304873.ref001","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"656","DOI":"10.1002\/j.1538-7305.1949.tb00928.x","article-title":"Communication theory of secrecy systems","volume":"28","author":"CE Shannon","year":"1949","journal-title":"The Bell system technical journal"},{"issue":"10","key":"pone.0304873.ref002","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"e0293418","DOI":"10.1371\/journal.pone.0293418","article-title":"A double encryption protection algorithm for stem cell bank privacy data based on improved AES and chaotic encryption technology","volume":"18","author":"L Wang","year":"2023","journal-title":"Plos one"},{"issue":"1","key":"pone.0304873.ref003","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"101928","DOI":"10.1016\/j.jksuci.2024.101928","article-title":"DRcipher: A pseudo-random dynamic round lightweight block cipher","volume":"36","author":"J Kuang","year":"2024","journal-title":"Journal of King Saud University-Computer and Information Sciences"},{"issue":"9","key":"pone.0304873.ref004","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"e0291429","DOI":"10.1371\/journal.pone.0291429","article-title":"On the dynamic reconfigurable implementations of MISTY1 and KASUMI block ciphers.","volume":"18","author":"H Jiexian","year":"2023","journal-title":"Plos one."},{"key":"pone.0304873.ref005","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"11667","DOI":"10.1007\/s11042-019-08464-6","article-title":"S-box design based on optimize LFT parameter selection: a practical approach in recommendation system domain","volume":"79","author":"S Beg","year":"2020","journal-title":"Multimedia Tools and Applications"},{"key":"pone.0304873.ref006","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"136736","DOI":"10.1109\/ACCESS.2020.3010615","article-title":"Construction of Nonlinear Component of Block Cipher by Action of Modular Group PSL(2,Z) on Projective Line PL (GF(2,8)).","volume":"8","author":"W Gao","year":"2020","journal-title":"IEEE Access."},{"issue":"5","key":"pone.0304873.ref007","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"249","DOI":"10.1016\/0020-0190(95)00156-6","article-title":"Resistance of balanced s-boxes to linear and differential cryptanalysis","volume":"56","author":"AM Youssef","year":"1995","journal-title":"Information Processing Letters"},{"issue":"11","key":"pone.0304873.ref008","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"e0241890","DOI":"10.1371\/journal.pone.0241890","article-title":"A highl